I've finished all five work items: grade now measures document size without an LLM, records it, and turns over-limit values into findings. No real grade has been run yet, so I can't say how scores will actually move.

**What changed**
- **Metrics:** the new `src/grade/size-metrics.ts` counts the body's non-whitespace characters, leaving out table separator rows and finding comments. `grade apply` stores four things in the result's new `size_metrics` field:
  - the character count;
  - the ratio to the declared sample (deliverables only);
  - the growth since the previous grade;
  - the share of text under headings about verifying or operating the document. Each line counts under its innermost heading, so a mapping section inside a "…と検証" chapter doesn't count.
- **Findings:** a metric over its limit adds a minor or major finding to `vp-arc-conciseness`. It only lowers the score if the agent also points out removable text there (minor or higher). Otherwise it drops to `note`, so length alone never costs points. The grade plan now shows the metrics to the agent and keeps metric findings out of "前回の指摘".
- **Limits:** they live in `grade_rubric.size_limits` in `docs/ja/specdojo/defaults/pm-review-viewpoints.yaml`, so a project can change them. The values are set from the PJR-8S4T and PJR-CPZH/Y5AC measurements (table below). I added matching schema entries and a value check.
- **Rubric version:** I changed the id to `grade-rubric-v3`, so `--rubric-outdated` selects all v2 results. Weights and the pass score are unchanged. Old results without `size_metrics` still load.
- **`vp-arc-conciseness` wording:** it now treats restating the common reference documents and re-saying the same judgment in several chapters as redundant. It allows major when about 20% or more could be cut.
- **Docs:** updated review-guide and command-reference.

**Default limits**

| Metric | minor | major | Minimum amount |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Ratio to the sample | 1.5× | 2.5× | 1,000 more characters than the sample |
| Growth since the last grade | 1.3× | 1.8× | 1,000 more characters |
| Verification/operations share | 20% | 30% | 500 characters in those sections |

**Checked against real documents (measured, then pinned in unit tests)**

| Document | Measured | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Comparison of alternatives after PJR-CPZH (before the rewrite) | 3.28× sample, 2.21× growth | major, major |
| Quality management plan now (after PJR-Y5AC) | 1.72× sample, 3.64× growth | minor, major |
| sld-sample before PJR-8S4T | 29% verification share | minor |
| sld-sample now | 10% | not flagged |

sld-sample's current version was already slimmed by PJR-8S4T and passes at 96. I treated it as the case that should *not* be flagged; the ticket's wording could be read as expecting it to be caught.

**Things to know**
- **Growth starts late:** it only works once a document has a v3 result with `size_metrics`.
- **Inline-mode grades** (written into the document body) don't get the metrics; only the current sidecar results do.
- **Re-grading load:** switching to v3 means the routine with `rubric_outdated: true` will gradually re-grade every v2 result, which uses agent quota.
- **Lint warnings:** ESLint's function-length warnings in `apply.ts` and `plan.ts` were already there; I added 1–2 lines to each.
- **No protected config changed.** I didn't touch `CHANGELOG.md`; the proposed entry is in the ticket.
- **Size numbers:** my character counts run a little under PJR-8S4T's (9,648 vs 9,885 for the old sld-sample).

**Tests run:** the 15 new unit tests and the existing grade/review/docs tests pass. I also ran `tsc`, ESLint, Prettier, markdownlint, schema validation and `validate:history-links` (exit 0); the runner will rerun its own checks.

The ticket's results section has the full reasoning.

